{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "log-02-product-quotient-power-laws",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Product, Quotient, and Power Laws of Logarithms",
    "description": "Combine and split logarithms using the product, quotient, and power laws, including log_a a = 1 and log_a 1 = 0.",
    "teachingApproach": "Build each law from powers with the same base, then immediately use it on a fresh expression. Contrast products with sums so learners do not invent a false 'log of a sum' rule. Finish by combining several laws and the inverse relation a^(log_a x)=x.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 28,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 10, Logarithms. Teach log_a(xy)=log_a x+log_a y, log_a(x/y)=log_a x−log_a y, and log_a(x^p)=p log_a x from concrete powers. Cover log_a a=1, log_a1=0, and a^(log_a x)=x. Train learners to combine several terms into one logarithm before calculating, to move coefficients into exponents, and to spot the classic false rule log(x+y)=log x+log y. Include simplified symbolic forms and common-log examples where products become powers of 10."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's make logarithms easier by combining them.\n\nIf two logarithms have the same base, products, quotients, and powers can be handled without evaluating every log separately.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Start with a product.\n\nlog₂8 + log₂4 = 3 + 2 = 5.\n\nBut 8×4=32, and log₂32=5 too.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "That pattern is the product law:\n\nlog_a(xy)=log_a x + log_a y\n\nAdding logs of the same base is the same as multiplying their arguments.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "q_1": {
      "id": "q_1",
      "type": "CHOICE",
